    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d99">

                <pb facs="0113" n="99" xml:id="pg99"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense(M)/12009: Airgram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Scotten</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">San
                                José</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-02-09T10:11:00-06:00">February 9, 1943—10:11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 11—5 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–411. Referring to my telegram No. 99, February 8, 12 noon, the Minister<anchor
                        n="18" xml:id="d99-f1"/> points out that in article 2 the statement “shall
                    be free to dispose of its personal property, machinery and equipment” if
                    interpreted literally might mean that at the end of 25 years the Costa Rican
                    Government would not receive the plantation in running order. He hopes that a
                    change can be made to ensure that Costa Rica will receive the machinery and
                    equipment at the end of 25 years. He requests a definition of “personal
                    property”.</p>
                <p>At the end of article 5 the Minister would appreciate the addition of something
                    along the following lines: “Costa Rica reserves the right, however, to withhold
                    from exportation sufficient quantities of anti-malarial products to supply its
                    normal domestic consumption”.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Scotten</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d99-f1" n="18" xml:id="d99-f1-content">
